Keep the channel's servers when the system config names no usable one.

A resolver config can hold nameserver lines that c-ares drops while it
parses them: a link-local address with no interface, or a blacklisted
IPv6 address. The parsed list is then empty but not absent, and
ares_sysconfig_apply() hands it to ares_servers_update(), which removes
every server the channel has. A channel with no server answers
ARES_ENOSERVER for every query after that, for the life of the channel.

An absent list (a config file that names no server at all) already
leaves the servers alone, because sysconfig->sconfig stays NULL. This
patch gives an empty list the same treatment.

ares_init_options() is unaffected: a new channel has no server yet, so
it adds its default server exactly as before. The change is visible
only to ares_reinit() on a live channel.

--- a/src/lib/ares_sysconfig.c
+++ b/src/lib/ares_sysconfig.c
@@ -517,7 +517,11 @@
 {
   ares_status_t status;
 
-  if (sysconfig->sconfig && !(channel->optmask & ARES_OPT_SERVERS)) {
+  /* Bun: an empty list means the config named only servers that could not be
+   * used. Keep the ones the channel has: a channel with no server fails every
+   * query from here on. */
+  if (ares_llist_len(sysconfig->sconfig) > 0 &&
+      !(channel->optmask & ARES_OPT_SERVERS)) {
     status = ares_servers_update(channel, sysconfig->sconfig, ARES_FALSE);
     if (status != ARES_SUCCESS) {
       return status;
